<td id="S5.F18.3.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding:-4pt 0.2pt;"><img src="/html/1611.07004/assets/figs/shoes_sketches_lotsofresults_latex/L1cGAN_14971.jpg" id="S5.F18.g24" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" width="115" height="115" alt="Refer to caption"></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F18.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 18</span>: </span><span id="S5.F18.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Additional results of the edges<math id="S5.F18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>photo models applied to human-drawn sketches from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">eitz2012humans</a></cite>. Note that the models were trained on automatically detected edges, but generalize to human drawings</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F19" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="/html/1611.07004/assets/figs/inpaint1.png" id="S5.F19.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" width="336" height="266" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="/html/1611.07004/assets/figs/inpaint2.png" id="S5.F19.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" width="336" height="266" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F19.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 19</span>: </span><span id="S5.F19.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Example results on photo inpainting, compared to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">pathak2016context</a></cite>, on the Paris StreetView dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">doersch2012makes</a></cite>. This experiment demonstrates that the U-net architecture can be effective even when the predicted pixels are not geometrically aligned with the information in the input – the information used to fill in the central hole has to be found in the periphery of these photos.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
